Why Are Interposers Important to Advanced Packaging?

An interposer sits between semiconductor dies and the package substrate or other interconnect structures. It provides electrical routing that allows multiple components to communicate within a compact package.

Silicon interposers can support dense interconnections, while glass and organic approaches offer different manufacturing, electrical and cost characteristics. The appropriate technology depends on the required interconnect density, package dimensions, signal performance and production economics.

Interposers are particularly relevant to advanced packages that combine logic and memory or integrate multiple functional dies.

AI Accelerators and High-Bandwidth Memory Support Demand

AI accelerators and high-performance computing processors require fast communication between processing units and memory. Advanced packaging can shorten interconnect distances and support high-density connections between dies.

Interposer-based designs can help meet these requirements by providing a platform for dense routing. The growth of chiplet architectures is also increasing interest in packaging methods that allow different dies to be combined within one system.

Adoption depends on package cost, manufacturing yield, thermal management and the complexity of the interconnect architecture.

Silicon, Glass and Other Interposer Technologies

Silicon interposers have been used in high-density packaging because they can support fine-pitch routing. Glass interposers are being developed for applications that may benefit from their electrical characteristics and dimensional stability.

Organic interposers and related packaging structures can offer alternative cost and manufacturing trade-offs. No single approach fits every application, and designers must consider the complete package rather than the interposer material alone.

